|}

==Models of occupancy and stewardship==

Instead of a market dominated by private, individual ownership or rental agreements, housing across Balisca is organized around two primary, non-speculative models of occupancy.

===Housing cooperatives (co-ops)===
[[File:Alterra co-op.jpg|thumb|right|220px|''Alterra'', a [[w:Housing cooperative|cooperative housing]] and autonomous commune in [[Syracuse]] ([[Piedmont]])]]

Housing cooperatives are the most common housing model in all urban and semi-urban areas of Balisca, accounting for approximately 78% of Balisca's existing housing stock.

* '''Ownership:''' Residents do not "own" their individual apartment or unit. Instead, they own an equity share in the cooperative (co-op) that collectively owns and democratically manages the entire building, complex, or block.
* '''Management:''' The cooperative is managed by the residents themselves, who elect a council or make decisions through direct assemblies. They are responsible for the building's maintenance, budget, and rules.
* '''Payment:''' Residents pay a monthly fee or due (not "rent") to the cooperative. This fee is calculated to cover the collective costs of maintenance, utilities, communal investments (such as a new [[W:Urban farming|rooftop garden]] or childcare facility), and payments to the communal land trust. This fee is stable and based on ''cost'', not market value.

====Types of housing cooperatives====

Baliscan housing cooperatives take several forms:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Type !! Description !! Prevalence
|-
| '''Resident-owned co-ops''' || The most common type. Residents collectively own the building and land through their cooperative shares. || 52% of co-ops
|-
| '''Limited-equity co-ops''' || Residents purchase shares at below-market rates, with restrictions on resale value to maintain affordability. || 28% of co-ops
|-
| '''Leasehold co-ops''' || The co-op owns the building but leases the land from a communal land trust. || 15% of co-ops
|-
| '''Mutual housing associations''' || Larger-scale cooperatives that manage multiple buildings across a neighborhood or district. || 5% of co-ops
|}

====Formation and governance====

New housing cooperatives are formed through a standardized process:

# '''Organizing committee:''' A group of prospective residents forms an organizing committee, often with support from a cantonal housing authority or existing cooperative federation.
# '''Feasibility study:''' The committee works with architects, planners, and financial advisors to develop a proposal.
# '''Approval:''' The proposal is reviewed by the cantonal housing authority for compliance with planning regulations and housing standards.
# '''Construction or acquisition:''' The cooperative either constructs new housing or acquires existing housing (from previous cooperatives or through public programs).
# '''Incorporation:''' The cooperative is formally incorporated under the Cooperative Housing Code.
# '''Resident selection:''' New members are selected based on need, with priority given to those in inadequate housing, large families, seniors, and people with disabilities.

Governance typically follows one of two models:

* '''Council model:''' Residents elect a governing council that makes day-to-day decisions, with major decisions requiring approval by the full membership.
* '''Assembly model:''' All decisions are made by the full membership in regular assemblies, with committees handling implementation.

====Financial structure====

The financial structure of Baliscan housing cooperatives is designed to ensure stability and prevent speculation:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Component !! Description
|-
| '''Share price''' || The initial purchase price of a cooperative share, typically equivalent to 2–5 years of monthly fees. This is returned when a resident leaves the cooperative.
|-
| '''Monthly fee''' || Covers operating costs, maintenance reserves, payments to the communal land trust, and capital improvements. Typically 15–25% of median household income.
|-
| '''Capital reserves''' || Mandatory reserves for major repairs and replacements, typically 1–2% of the cooperative's annual budget.
|-
| '''Communal land trust payment''' || Annual payment to the communal land trust that owns the underlying land, typically 0.5–1% of the cooperative's budget.
|}

===Communal tenure===

This model is common in more autonomous communes and more sparsely populated areas, particularly in [[Rural society in Balisca|rural regions]] of [[Araucaria]] and throughout the indigenous territories of [[Pindorama]]. Approximately 18% of Balisca's housing stock is under communal tenure.

* '''Ownership:''' The housing stock is owned entirely by the commune as a collective good.
* '''Allocation:''' Units are allocated to individuals and families directly by the communal assembly, based on demonstrated need, family size, or new arrivals.
* '''Payment:''' Residents often pay no direct fee, as the {{W|Building cooperative|housing's construction and maintenance}} are considered part of the commune's collective labor and social obligations, funded by the commune's overall productive output.

====Indigenous housing models====

Indigenous communities in Balisca maintain traditional housing practices that predate and coexist with the cooperative model:

* '''[[Wallche people|Wallche]] traditional housing:''' Extended family compounds organized around communal spaces, with construction and maintenance governed by traditional customs.
* '''[[Kurupiranga]] malocas:''' Large communal houses that serve as both residential and ceremonial spaces, housing multiple families under one roof.
* '''[[Araucarian]] ruka:''' Traditional wooden houses built using indigenous techniques, often clustered in small settlements with shared agricultural land.

These indigenous models are protected by the [[Indigenous Housing Rights Act of 1995]], which recognizes traditional land tenure systems and exempts indigenous communities from certain provisions of the Confederal Housing Act.

===Specialized housing models===

====Student housing====

Students in Balisca are housed through specialized cooperative housing operated by or in partnership with educational institutions. Student housing cooperatives typically feature:

* Shared common facilities (kitchens, study areas, recreation rooms)
* Individual or shared bedrooms
* Subsidized monthly fees (often 50–75% of standard cooperative rates)
* Flexible lease terms aligned with academic calendars

====Senior housing====

Housing for seniors (65+) is organized through specialized cooperatives and communal arrangements that provide:

* Age-appropriate accessibility features
* On-site healthcare and support services
* Community programming and social activities
* Integration with broader community life (avoiding isolation)

Senior housing cooperatives account for approximately 8% of the cooperative housing stock.

====Supportive housing====

For individuals with disabilities, mental health challenges, or histories of housing instability, Balisca provides supportive housing that combines:

* Permanent, stable housing
* On-site support services (case management, healthcare, job training)
* Small-scale, community-integrated settings
* Resident participation in governance

====Emergency and transitional housing====

The confederal government maintains a network of emergency shelters and transitional housing for those in immediate need:

* '''Emergency shelters:''' Short-term accommodation (up to 30 days) for those without housing, providing basic necessities and connection to permanent housing services.
* '''Transitional housing:''' Medium-term accommodation (up to 2 years) for those transitioning from homelessness, institutionalization, or crisis situations.
* '''Rapid rehousing:''' Programs that quickly place individuals and families into permanent cooperative housing with temporary financial support.

==Architecture and design standards==
{{Further information|Architecture of Balisca}}Baliscan housing is characterized by distinctive architectural principles that reflect the confederation's values of sustainability, community, and human dignity.

===Ecological urbanism===
Baliscan housing design is guided by principles of ecological urbanism, formalized through the [[Low Impact Initiative]] (1977/1983) and subsequent legislation: 

* '''Integration with nature:''' Buildings are designed to minimize environmental impact and integrate with surrounding ecosystems.
* '''Pedestrian priority:''' Neighborhoods are designed for walking, with cars relegated to the periphery or underground.
* '''Mixed use:''' Residential, commercial, and community facilities are integrated within walking distance.
* '''Public transit orientation:''' Higher-density development is concentrated around public transit nodes.
* '''Green infrastructure:''' Parks, gardens, and green corridors are integrated into all developments.

The [[Casajuan Plan]] (2019) has reinforced and updated these principles for contemporary challenges faced by urban areas, particularly addressing [[W:Urban decay|urban decay]] since the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Balisca]] and the conversion of vacant commercial space into new social housing developments.

===Sustainable building materials===
