|}

==Financing and construction==

===How housing is funded===

Housing in Balisca is funded through a combination of public investment, cooperative equity, and communal resources:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Source !! Contribution !! Mechanism
|-
| '''Confederal Housing Fund''' || 40–60% of construction costs || Grants and low-interest loans to cooperatives
|-
| '''Cantonal contributions''' || 10–20% of construction costs || Matching grants and infrastructure investment
|-
| '''Communal land trust''' || Land value || Land is provided at no cost or nominal lease
|-
| '''Cooperative equity''' || 20–40% of construction costs || Share purchases by future residents
|-
| '''Communal labor''' || Variable || Contribution of labor reduces costs
|}

The [[Confederal Housing Fund]] is financed through:

* Confederal budget allocation (approximately 3% of confederal spending)
* Taxes on commercial property and business activities
* Revenue from natural resource extraction
* Repayments from mature cooperatives

===Construction cooperatives===

Many housing cooperatives are built by [[W:Building cooperatives|construction cooperatives]]—worker-owned enterprises that specialize in sustainable construction. Construction cooperatives:

* Are owned and democratically managed by construction workers
* Prioritize worker safety, fair wages, and skill development
* Use sustainable materials and techniques
* Often partner with housing cooperatives for ongoing maintenance

===Public works programs===

Since the Revolution, and expanding since the [[Special Period]] and the onset of the housing shortage, the confederal government has allocated increased resources towards [[W:Public works programs|public works programs]] ('''pwp''', usually through the [[Baliscan Selective Service]]) with the aim of:

* Training workers in construction skills
* Providing employment while addressing housing shortages
* Prioritizing housing for program participants
* Integrating pwp with broader infrastructure development

===Maintenance and renovation===

Ongoing maintenance is funded through cooperative monthly fees, which include:

* '''Operating costs:''' Utilities, cleaning, security, administration
* '''Maintenance reserves:''' Funds set aside for repairs and replacements
* '''Capital improvements:''' Sinking funds for major renovations
* '''Professional services:''' Contracts with maintenance cooperatives or specialists

Major renovations may receive additional funding from:

* Confederal renovation grants
* Energy efficiency incentives
* Accessibility improvement programs

==Rights and protections==

===Security of tenure===

Baliscan residents enjoy strong security of tenure:

* '''Right to remain:''' Residents cannot be evicted except for serious cause (non-payment, severe rule violations, or demolition).
* '''Due process:''' All evictions require approval by a cantonal housing tribunal.
* '''Alternative accommodation:''' Even in cases of eviction, the resident must be provided with alternative housing.
* '''Inheritance:''' Cooperative shares can be transferred to family members under certain conditions.

===Protection against displacement===

When housing must be demolished or substantially renovated:

* Residents have the right to return to equivalent housing in the same area.
* Temporary accommodation is provided at no cost during displacement.
* Moving expenses are covered by the responsible party.
* Residents participate in planning for new housing.

===Right to housing===

Any person without housing can present themselves to a cantonal housing office and receive:

* Immediate emergency shelter (same day)
* Assessment of housing needs
* Placement on waiting lists for permanent housing
* Support services as needed

The maximum wait time for permanent housing is legally limited to 180 days, though current shortages have extended this in some areas.

===Appeals process===

Residents who believe their housing rights have been violated can appeal to:

* '''Cooperative grievance committee:''' Internal resolution mechanism
* '''Cantonal housing office:''' Administrative review
* '''[[Confederal Council of Arbitration|Consejo Confederal de Arbitrage]]:''' Final arbiter of housing disputes
* '''Constitutional Tribunal:''' For claims of constitutional violations

