===Model communities===

Several communities serve as models for Baliscan housing principles:

* '''[[Alterra Commune]]:''' A large-scale cooperative that functions as an autonomous commune, with its own schools, healthcare, and democratic governance.
* '''[[Vila Ecológica]]:''' A model ecological community demonstrating net-zero energy housing and closed-loop resource systems.
* '''[[Comunitat Indígena de Pindorama]]:''' An indigenous community that successfully integrates traditional housing with modern sustainability practices.

==Challenges and criticism==

===Ongoing housing shortage===
{{Further information|Baliscan housing shortage }}The most significant challenge facing Baliscan housing is the current shortage, which emerged in the 2010s:

* '''Causes:''' High immigration rates, urbanization, demographic changes, and construction capacity constraints
* '''Symptoms:''' Long waiting lists for housing, overcrowding in some units, pressure on emergency shelters
* '''Response:''' Increased construction funding, streamlined approval processes, innovative density solutions

The shortage is a crisis of scarcity rather than affordability—those with housing pay stable, affordable fees, but obtaining housing in the first place can involve long waits.

===Criticism from various perspectives===

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Perspective !! Criticism !! Baliscan Response
|-
| '''Market advocates''' || Lack of consumer choice; inability to build wealth through property || Housing is for living, not speculation; other mechanisms exist for wealth-building
|-
| '''Individualists''' || Collective decision-making constrains individual freedom || Democratic participation is empowering; residents have more control than tenants in market systems
|-
| '''Efficiency advocates''' || Construction is slow; bureaucracy impedes development || Deliberate process ensures quality and community input; speed is not the only metric
|-
| '''Traditionalists''' || Modern architecture doesn't reflect traditional styles || New construction incorporates traditional elements; historic preservation is valued
|-
| '''Density skeptics''' || High-density living is undesirable || Density enables sustainability, transit, and community; quality design makes density livable
|}

===Reform proposals===

Various proposals have been made to address current challenges:

* '''Accelerated construction:''' Increased funding for construction cooperatives and public works programs
* '''Innovative density:''' Allow greater height and density in transit corridors
* '''Modular construction:''' Use prefabricated components to speed construction
* '''Adaptive reuse:''' Convert commercial buildings to housing where appropriate
* '''Regional development:''' Encourage development in smaller cities and rural areas to reduce pressure on major urban centers

